# Player-facing consistency

A new screen, dot command, or Options row uses a shape that already exists.
Pick one. Do not invent a parallel chrome, a second grammar, or a new menu
style.

If this rule did not attach and the player will see the change, follow it anyway.

## Windows

Seven kits. `EditorDialogChrome` only sets size and theme. The words below
are still the caller's job. Title treatment is `BlowTorch.Chrome.Title`
(42dip, dark bar). Do not paste a new hex colour.

| Kit | When | Chrome |
|---|---|---|
| List | Pick or create a row, then leave | Full screen. Footer: one create verb, optional Refresh, `?`, More, **Close**. Search just above the footer, or none. |
| Form | Write one thing | **Cancel** discards, optional `?`, **Done** writes and closes. Long form full screen. Short form wrap-height. |
| Read | Read, not save | **Close**, plus only text navigation (Back, Older, Newer). Search under the title. Help and session logs are full screen. The small `?` card (`EditorHelp`) stays wrap-height. |
| Overlay window | A window on the game | Sentence-case title, drag, optional gear, `ic_window_close`. No Done. Recent commands and the suggestion list are this. |
| Chip strip | Chips over the game, or a strip in the layout | Grip and chips. No title. Suggestion float, recent-command float, and the recent-command bar are this. |
| Confirm | One question | Themed `BlowTorch_Dialog`. **Cancel** and one verb. A message you only acknowledge uses **Close**. |
| Measure | Wave the phone, read a number | One verb and **Close**. |

**Done** means write. **Close** means leave. **Cancel** means discard.
**Send** is only the MCP editor that transmits.

`list`, `float`, and `bar` are places. `list` is the overlay window.
`float` is chips over the game. `bar` is the strip in the layout.
Suggestions: `.suggest where list|floating|bar|off`. The suggestion-list
gear sets opacity, font, lines, wrap, tappable, minimal frame, and keep
opacity. It does not set how many commands are kept. Recent commands:
`.lastlist` (the window), `.lastlist float` or `.lastfloat` (chips; `off`
hides them; the grip drags them on its own), `.lastlist bar`
(the same bar as `.lastbar`). Do not invent a fourth place name.

Mapper screens and Options sub-dialogs use these shells, not a platform
`AlertDialog`. A second action (renegotiate, Save for host) stays its own
button. Done does not start doing that action.

Search stays at the bottom of a list and under the title of a document.

## Dot commands

The `.commands` one-liner says what the bare name does, in the first clause.

- A saved on/off prints status and does not flip. The words are `on`, `off`,
  and `toggle`. This includes `.editpanel`, `.fullscreen`, `.map record`,
  `.map echo`, `.map oneway`, `.map gmcp`, and `.map gmcp grow`.
- A summon runs: `.lastlist`, `.search`, `.options`. `.chat` toggles;
  `.chat open` opens and stays open; `.chat close` closes and stays closed.
- A one-shot runs: `.ping`, `.disconnect`. Extra words are usage. They do
  not still run the action.
- A language with many verbs (`.map`, `.window`, `.widget`, `.trigger`,
  `.alias`, `.gmcp`, `.mcp`) prints a short status when one exists, otherwise
  usage. Opening stays a verb (`.map open`, `.window show`). `help` is the
  wall. `.trigger` has no `list`.

An unknown word, including a trailing word after a valid `on`, is the red
usage line. It is not sent, and it does not change state.

`true` / `false` / `1` / `0` / `yes` / `no` / `enable` / `disable` stay
working on commands that already accept them. Do not document them. Do not
add them to a new command.

`.pick` and `.grabber` stay modes. `on` means keep going, not a saved
preference.

A bare number keeps one meaning, and the one-liner states it. `.suggest 5`
takes chip 5. `.suggest show 5` is how many are offered. `.lastbar 5` is
how many chips, and it turns the bar on. `.lastfloat 5` is how many
recent-command chips, and it shows them. `.lastfloat length 12` is how
many characters of each (3–40). `.lastlist float 5` and `.lastlist float
length 12` are the same.

`.suggest where floating|bar|list|off` is where suggestions sit. `.suggest
where next` steps floating, bar, list, off. The first step from floating
is still the bar. `.lastlist frame` hides that one window's title, gear,
and close. Do not add `frame` to a window that does not have that chrome.
`.lastlist float off` and `.lastfloat off` hide the recent-command chips.
`on` shows them. `toggle` flips. Dragging that grip moves the chips whether
or not suggestions are floating.

`.togglefullscreen` still flips on every call, so old buttons keep working.
New fullscreen control is `.fullscreen`.

## Options

Pages are drill-ins. A group's description is the one-line preview of what
is inside. Do not add an accordion.

Do not rename a settings key. Do not reorder a `ListOption`'s entries. The
saved value is the index.

A window key (`WindowToken.OPTION_KEY`) stays inside the main window
`SettingsGroup`. Moved out, neither saver writes it. A connection key may
sit on a root page or nested under Window. Both savers recurse and skip
foreign keys. Extra text and widgets live under Panes.

Put a new row on the page it belongs to: Text, Layout, Links, Input bar,
Typing, Suggestions, Gestures, Panes, Chat, Sound, Connection, Protocols,
Mapper, Device, Files. Button and Starter Tutorial stay the plugin pages
at the end.

A description is one sentence: what changes on screen or on the wire. No
paragraph. No dot-command tail.

A row hidden from the menu stays in the tree (`HIDDEN_OPTION_KEYS`), or it
stops being saved.

When a path the player can read changes, update `docs/user-manual.md` and
`BTLib/res/raw/user_manual.txt` together (they are the same file),
`docs/options-guide.md`, the starter tutorial, and the help string, in the
same change.
